Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Bridge Buzzes Before the Sea Turns Gold
- Story & Context: The EP opens at 94 BPM with no kick. Clara bows a low tromba-marina fundamental and moves through its fifth and octave harmonics while the bridge produces a dry, papery rattle beneath each tone. Leila enters first with fingertip frame-drum taps and a loose shaker pattern. Irene waits until the third harmonic cycle before sliding upward into a low Dorian pedal from nearly a semitone below.

Noah adds one Rhodes add9 voicing every four bars, each chord short enough that the instrument never becomes a pad. Mateu enters only after Clara reaches a brighter upper partial, playing a two-note cup-muted flugelhorn phrase that sounds almost like the harmonic has acquired lungs. The first kick does not appear until nearly ninety seconds into the track.

The central section introduces the Missing-Kick Coastline rule: after four bars of full downtempo pulse, the kick disappears for another four while bass release, frame drum, and bow sustain keep the groove intact. At the peak, Clara increases bow pressure until the bridge buzz nearly overtakes the pitch, then abruptly backs away to a clean harmonic. The track ends with the room microphone capturing distant sea wind through the shutters after every instrument has stopped.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Late-afternoon Mediterranean equipment room with a tall dark tromba marina beside an open sea-facing shutter, golden light striking the vibrating bridge, faded frequency chart curling from the wall, fretless bass and muted flugelhorn resting in soft shadow. No full lyrics; optional sparse spoken fragments limited to “buzz before gold,” “leave the kick out,” “hold the fifth,” and “let the air come through.”

Track 2: Bearing 247 Has No Downbeat
- Story & Context: Noah builds the most rhythmically deceptive piece on the EP from a 101 BPM pattern whose first kick consistently lands after beat one. Leila reinforces the ambiguity with frame-drum heel strokes grouped 3+3+2 across the bar. Clara plays a repeating overtone cell that begins in the same place each cycle but feels displaced because the drum pattern refuses to confirm a conventional downbeat.

Irene uses a fretless line of only three pitches, but every approach changes: one glides upward, one falls into place, one arrives cleanly. Mateu answers the longest slides with soft minor-sixth intervals. Noah occasionally opens a Juno-style chorus on one electric-piano dyad for a single bar, then collapses the stereo width again.

Halfway through, all drum-machine elements disappear. Leila keeps the 101 BPM pulse acoustically while Clara and Irene continue the same phrase. When the machine returns, it enters on the same displaced kick pattern rather than correcting the listener’s sense of one. The final eight bars contain no kick at all, only frame drum, bowed buzz, bass harmonics, and one flugelhorn exhale.
